# D31's second novel-diagnostic demonstration: D19's generator self-diagnosis
# (SelfDiagnosis.saturated / LoadReport.inconclusive, R11). Unlike the back-off warning
# (checkout-burst.tflw), this one is deliberately **not** a comparison against k6 — it's specific
# to tflw's own single-process Node generator being the bottleneck, a concept k6's compiled Go
# generator doesn't have an equivalent failure mode for (D19's own framing: "almost no load tool
# tells you this"). No real API target needed either: this isolates D19 on its own rather than
# diluting it with perf-0's network variability, the same reasoning
# packages/cli/test/e2e.test.ts's own inconclusive-exit-code test uses.

use "./helpers.ts"

test "generator self-saturation demo"
  ramp to 8 users over 3s
  let burned = burnCpu()
  threshold p95 duration is less than 100000ms
  # Required as of tflw M89c (TF033): a `duration` threshold must be paired with an unscoped
  # `error rate` one, because a duration threshold reads only the iterations that succeeded and so
  # cannot observe failure on its own. Here it is close to unfalsifiable — this test has no `api`
  # step at all, so the only way an iteration fails is `burnCpu()` throwing. That is tflw's own
  # `B3-17`: the checker can require the line, only the author can make it mean something. It costs
  # nothing here: a saturated run marks *every* threshold `skipped`, which is this file's whole point.
  threshold error rate is less than 1%
